WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.720
Data exposure can happen when sensitive fields are stored in plaintext,

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:09.380
sent without transport protection, logged carelessly, cached in unsafe places,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.920
or returned to clients that do not need them.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:16.780
Weak encryption is often a design and implementation problem rather than a single missing

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:18.120
library call.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:23.140
Outdated algorithms, hard-coded secrets, custom crypto, poor randomness,

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.320
and weak password hashing all reduce the protection that encryption is supposed to

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:29.500
provide.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:33.560
Missing or misconfigured TLS leaves credentials, session tokens,

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:37.300
and business data exposed to interception or downgrade attacks.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.720
Certificate validation, protocol versions,

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.620
and secure defaults must be handled consistently across services.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:49.240
Key management is part of the control, not an administrative afterthought.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:54.580
Keys need protected storage, rotation, access limits, lifecycle management,

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.040
and separation from the data they protect.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:01.500
Use strong, approved cryptographic algorithms and vetted libraries.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:05.080
Protect data in transit with properly configured TLS.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:10.740
Store keys in managed secret or key management systems with rotation and access controls.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:16.960
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include SC-12 - Cryptographic Key Establishment and

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:18.060
Management;

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:24.600
SC-13 - Cryptographic Protection; SC-28 - Protection of Information at Rest.
